# Romance-specific hook-writing guidance. Appended after the language guidance by
# prompts/hook_prompt.py::build_hook_prompt. Additive craft notes only.

ROMANCE_GUIDANCE = """
    ────────────────────
    ROMANCE-SPECIFIC GUIDANCE
    ────────────────────

    THIS SECTION OVERRIDES EVERYTHING ABOVE IT, the language section included, whenever they disagree — lean into the distance between the leads, not a plot-shaped reading of the same scene.

    ROMANCE VOICE (HIGHEST PRIORITY IN THIS SECTION): write the hook so the reader FEELS what the acting lead feels in the body, not so they merely follow what happens. Let the wording carry the pull: desire, longing, the rush of being near, a racing heart, butterflies, heat rising, a breath that will not steady, knees going weak, wanting what they swore not to want. The feeling comes from WHAT THE BODY DOES in the act (a hand tightening, eyes dropping to lips, a breath caught, a step that will not move away), not from heavy or fancy words. Use only plain, everyday, simple words, the same ones the story uses; DO NOT reach for complex, literary or ornate vocabulary to sound romantic. Make every sentence of a closeness beat carry the lead's FEELING, not just the act.
    • THE EMOTION IS THE CONTENT. Work out what the acting lead is feeling in this moment from the TARGET SCENE and the DOMINANT EMOTION notes (longing, desire, jealousy, tenderness, vulnerability, possessiveness, heartbreak) and let the wording carry it, heightened and dramatic. DO NOT narrate the beat as a task the leads are performing (he holds her, she turns, he leans in) with no feeling inside it. Every action sentence has the feeling in it: what the lead is aching for, fighting, afraid of or cannot stop wanting, said as the lead lives it, in plain words. Give it detail and drama, never a list of movements. This voice outranks the plain-reporting and neutral-description cautions above; it does not outrank source fidelity or the plain-words rule in SPEAK THE STORY'S OWN LANGUAGE.
    • IRRESISTIBILITY IS THE THREAD THROUGH EVERY ROMANCE HOOK. Wherever the scene allows, word the lead's feeling as a pull they cannot resist: they cannot look away, cannot stay where they are, cannot stop themselves, lose the fight, are drawn in whatever they swore. Put it in the ground sentence's stance, in the beat and in the close, in different plain words each time. Match it to the scene: in a care or comfort scene the pull is tenderness they cannot hold back, in a jealous one it is the want they cannot hide, in a teasing one it is the flustered pull they cannot play down. The two leads feel it both ways when the scene shows both; otherwise the lead who feels it carries it. DO NOT invent a pull the scene and the DOMINANT EMOTION do not support.
    • EVERY PRECAP HINTS AT THE CLOSENESS STILL AHEAD. Even when the precap is only building toward a close moment (a kiss, a night together, a confession, one finally reaching for the other), it must keep a live hint that the closeness is coming and leave open whether it will happen: the want the lead cannot hide, the line they are close to crossing, the step still between them. Hint it through the lead's pull and what is at stake, never by stating the result. The hint points at the step AFTER what this precap shows, so a beat already shown stays settled and is not asked about again. DO NOT answer the hint (it happens, it does not), and DO NOT leave a precap on such a scene with no hint at all.
    • THE PULL COMES BEFORE THE GESTURE. Name what is overwhelming the lead in that moment (her face, her laugh, the way she stands there, his nearness) and let the gesture follow as something they could not help. Write the lead stunned, swept, unable to hold back, wanting; then the touch. DO NOT write a gesture as a calm, tidy deed done with no pull behind it (he gently tied her hair, he held her shoulders) with the want left out. The same gesture means nothing without the want behind it.
    • DO NOT close on a vague mood line (silence was left between them, something unspoken remained). The close names what the lead is fighting or wants, or asks why they did it.
    • Keep the ground sentence SHORT, about 12 words or fewer, and spend the saved words on the beat itself.
    • Show the feeling through the lead's body and senses (eyes that will not move, lips, breath, pulse, a flush, hands that will not stay still), and use the story's own everyday words for them. Prefer the specific physical fact the scene gives (where a hand rests: waist, shoulder, cheek, hair, back; how far apart their faces are; where the eyes linger) over a general romantic phrase. One strong detail beats five generic ones.
    • Write from the desire of the lead who feels it: what pulls them toward the other and what holds them back. DO NOT state the feeling as a flat label ("she felt so much love"); make the reader live it through the body, the hesitation and what the lead cannot stop wanting.
    • The strongest beat is the incomplete one: the instant before a touch or kiss, just after unexpected closeness, a gesture cut off, or a lead pulling away while wanting to stay.
    • Match the angle to the scene: physical attraction, tender care, longing, jealousy, forbidden pull or playful teasing. DO NOT force lust into a scene of care, comfort or distress; there the pull is tenderness, and touch (a hold, a hand in the hair) is still shown.
    • DO NOT present a dream, fantasy or imagined moment as real, and DO NOT describe a non-consensual act as pleasurable or romantic.
    • PHYSICAL TOUCH IS NEVER PROHIBITED. When the scene's beat has a touch (a hand on a waist, an arm around her, fingers in hair, a body pressed close, a hold, a kiss on the forehead), show it plainly and vividly as already happening, with the want behind it. DO NOT leave it out, blur it into a mood or hold it back for a later precap; only what WHAT THIS PRECAP MUST HOLD BACK reserves for a later step stays back. The only thing to avoid is a graphic, explicit account; a touch itself is always allowed.
    • DO NOT give a lead a feeling or reaction that neither the scene nor the DOMINANT EMOTION supports, and DO NOT invent an action, kiss or confession to feed the mood.
    • DO NOT write the same sensory image or feeling as the precap before this one: rotate between eyes, breath, pulse, touch, hesitation, heat and teasing. DO NOT let one stock romantic phrase become a template.

    EVERY romance hook must carry the DISTANCE between the two leads — even a DANGER or LIFE-UPENDED peak is framed through what it does to the bond between them, never as a bare plot event with no relationship stake. Before writing, place the target scene on this list and let it set what you lean into; these mirror the PEAK TYPES the scene was planned around, so matching the hook's angle to the type sharpens it instead of guessing blind. Ground it in CHAPTER JUST READ the same way: wherever the text allows, the anchor (see PICK THE ANCHOR above) should be something the acting lead feels or has decided about the other lead, not just any fact from that chapter.

    • COMING-TOGETHER (an almost-kiss, a confession on the tip of the tongue, one reaching for the other, forced into the same room or bed): lean into PROXIMITY, shown clearly. Show how close they are, the breath between them, the thing being said or done, as the limit permits. "Their faces are inches apart" is the hook. What happens next is the chapter.
    • GOING-APART (a walkout, a hardening misunderstanding, an ultimatum, a third party stepping between them, one deciding to leave): lean into the WIDENING GAP. Name what is about to come between them — the word, the person, the decision — and stop before it does. Never say the relationship ends, survives, or how either of them takes it.
    • DANGER (a threat arriving, a secret about to surface, harm closing in on one while the other may or may not know): lean into the GAP IN KNOWLEDGE as much as the threat itself — one lead walking toward danger the reader can see coming, or one lead about to learn something that changes everything. Never say whether the danger lands or how the other lead responds to it. DO NOT narrate the hidden thing as a plain fact, DO NOT name the form the proof takes, and DO NOT say it reaches the lead it is travelling toward: name who is standing in its path and what they still believe while it comes, and leave the rest to the chapter.
    • REALISATION (one of them realises love, affection, or a bond — a quiet, internal, permanent shift): this is the hardest type to hook on because nothing external happens. Lean into the MOMENT THE SHIFT HAPPENS as something seeable — a look held a second too long, a reaction they cannot explain to themselves, a thought that catches them off guard — never state the realisation in so many words ("she realises she loves him" is the outcome, not the hook) and never say what they do about it.
    • INTIMACY & CLOSENESS (a touch that lingers, a held gaze, a shared silence, one caring for the other, a guard coming down, a private moment neither planned): lean into the QUIET BETWEEN THEM as something seeable: a hand, a doorway, a light left on, a distance of one step. Carry it through the acting lead's own body, not just the position in the room — a breath that catches, a pulse that will not settle, eyes that will not look away however much they want them to. The strongest version is INVOLUNTARY: the lead fighting the pull and losing, especially when it crosses something they swore to themselves earlier (never to trust again, never to feel this). That losing fight, not the touch itself, is what gives the moment its charge. One lead carries the verbs and the other is who they are close to. EVERY precap for this scene shows the closeness clearly: the physical proximity between the leads — how near they are, what touches, what the acting lead's body does — is plainly on the page, never only a mood or a distance. Where the moment is explicit or graphic, soften it in the writing: a hand finding a waist, a breath caught, eyes closing, a forehead against a shoulder — suggestion through the chapter's own words for the body and the room, never an explicit or graphic account of the contact. Clear and subtle are the same target, not opposites. Show only the beat WHAT THIS PRECAP MUST HOLD BACK gives this rung, as already happening, continuing the scene from where the earlier precaps stopped. Whatever an earlier precap showed is now settled fact: DO NOT describe it again, DO NOT use its wording, and DO NOT ask whether it happens. DO NOT let the close ask whether the beat happened or what it means to them now. Where a beat's weight is what it will change, name that something now stands between them or has been said, never what. Build it in this order: ONE past-tense ground sentence naming the stance the acting lead stands in toward the other (a vow never to trust again, a marriage that is only an arrangement, a betrayal that closed them off), then a turn into the scene, then the beat itself as plain physical movement, then the close. Close by asking WHY they did it when that stance says they would not, with the close's wording making clear it was a pull they could not resist. The gesture is shown whole; the reason is what you withhold, and you withhold it by cutting the chapter's own explanation for it out of the precap. DO NOT add a clause stating a decision, or a feeling that arrives after the gesture because of it — but the other lead frozen inside the gesture is part of the beat, not an aftermath.
    • LIFE-UPENDED (a marriage, a contract, a move, a pregnancy, a family decision, a truth that can't be taken back): lean into the TERMS CHANGING — what is about to be fixed, signed, announced, or discovered that resets what either lead can count on. Never say how either of them responds to the change or what becomes of them afterward.

    CLOSENESS IS A TOOL, NOT THE POINT: physical and emotional proximity is what makes a romance hook feel alive, but it still has to anchor to something concrete from CHAPTER JUST READ and promise ONE seeable moment from the target scene — see STAY INSIDE THE SOURCE and THE PRECAP IS ALWAYS ABOUT THE HERO OR HEROINE above. A hook that is only mood ("the air between them is charged") with nothing concrete happening is vague, not romantic — ground every closeness in a specific thing, named plainly and in simple words: a hand, a doorway, a name almost spoken, a shirt still on her shoulders.

    THE WITHHELD THING IN ROMANCE IS USUALLY ONE OF: the step AFTER the touch this precap shows (the kiss, the confession); which way a wavering heart decides; what a look or silence means; who the leads choose when forced to. Treat these exactly as STAY INSIDE THE SOURCE and NAME THE MOMENT, NEVER ITS AFTERMATH already require — the moment arrives as an image on the threshold, never as its own resolution.
    """

